    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1686">

                <pb facs="1332" n="1322" sameAs="frus:frus1949v08/pg1322"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">125.991H/9–649: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Vice Consul at Kunming</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Lutkins</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kunming</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-09-06T10:00:00+07:00">September 6,
                            1949—10 a. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 6—1:57 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>148. Reurtel 25, 4th.<anchor n="59" xml:id="d1686-f1"/> Decision to leave Kunming
                    awaits developments South China (refDeptel 61, August 12,<anchor n="60"
                        xml:id="d1686-f2"/> repeated Canton 558). Am making no plans to leave this
                    time since do not expect turn over near future.</p>
                <p>CNAC, CAT and CATC received orders no flights in or out of Kunming September 5
                    and 6; awaiting orders re operations thereafter. Air France operating as usual.
                    CAT leaving five Americans here and hopes continue operations though abandoning
                    Kunming as major service base.</p>
                <p>Situation quiet. Unable get definite information re reported Nationalist military
                    movements but seems to be crisis arising from efforts Nationalist Government to
                    force Lu Han off fence. Two provincial delegates sent Chungking to discuss
                    matter with Generalissimo.<anchor n="61" xml:id="d1686-f3"/> Believe clash
                    unlikely and that crisis will pass with compromise probably giving Central
                    Government more power.</p>
                <p>Sent Department; repeated OffEmb Hong Kong 56, Chungking 4.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lutkins</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1686-f1" n="59" xml:id="d1686-f1-content">
                <p>Not found in Department of State files.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1686-f2" n="60" xml:id="d1686-f2-content">
                <p>Same as telegram No. 62 to Chungking, <ref target="#pg1314">p. 1314</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1686-f3" n="61" xml:id="d1686-f3-content">
                <p>Chiang Kai-shek who retired as President on January 21, 1949.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
